// WHAT EACH DGX SPARK UNIT IS CURRENTLY SPOKEN FOR BY, AS A SET OF IDENTITIES WITH THEIR CAUSES, AND
// WHICH OF THEM A GIVEN SERVING SUBJECT MAY THEREFORE USE.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS A SAFETY REPAIR RATHER THAN A REFINEMENT.
// gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection carried the fleet as `host_count: 8` and screened a
// candidate with `hosts_consumed(c) <= fleet.host_count`. A scalar cannot tell eight free hosts from
// eight of which five are spoken for -- and five of these eight WERE spoken for: the four hosts of the
// one claimed serving group, and the host the GLM canary is placed on -- so the one screen whose whole
// job is to rule shapes OUT was generous in exactly the direction DESIGN section 5 forbids, and
// generous silently, because nothing in a count can carry the fact that it was wrong. The repair is
// not a check placed beside the count: it is to delete the count and derive the SET, so that a shape
// too wide for the hosts that actually exist has no admitting arm to reach. The ledger row is
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_capacity_screen_counts_a_population_it_never_identifies.
//
// THE SET IS DERIVED, NEVER AUTHORED. Every host list this module could have written already exists
// somewhere that owns it, and restating one here would be the second authority DESIGN section 3
// forbids -- the copy that goes stale while the original moves. So the inputs are inhabited rather
// than transcribed: the unit population is gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement's reserved identities; the
// standing role commitments are gunbc.spark.cell_role's assignments; the hosts of the serving groups
// this repository actually claims are gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired's groups read through
// gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed's observed cage membership; the placed serving arms are
// gunbc.spark.serving_arm_placement's claims; the held reservations are
// gunbc.spark.llama_cpp_rpc_observed's undischarged internal-participant hosts; and the rulings that
// reserve a cage for a serving subject are gunbc.spark.serving_subject's reservations. This module
// owns one fact and one only: that those JOIN, and how.
//
// AND THE JOIN ALREADY EXISTED, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF WHY THIS FILE IS HERE.
// gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build had authored `spark_build_host_excluded` /
// `admissible_spark_build_hosts` -- the same subtraction over two of the same authorities, to answer
// where a container build may run. A second consumer arriving with the same question is precisely the
// moment the fold stops being one module's private helper. So the fold moved here at its root, the
// build lane consumes it, and neither answers for the other.
//
// TWO QUESTIONS, ONE CENSUS, AND THEY ARE ANSWERED BY DIFFERENT PROJECTIONS OF THE SAME STANDINGS.
//
// The first is the NEW-PLACEMENT question: which units may receive work that NOTHING has placed yet.
// The container build and the published-image probe ask it, and the answer reads only OBSERVED
// causes -- a role, a claimed group, a placed arm, a held reservation -- because those are the facts
// that something is already running or reserved on the machine. Its projection is
// `spark_unplaced_hosts` and its admission seam is `admit_unplaced_host_live` (an effect reads the current authority first; `admit_unplaced_host` is the pure projection over the source rows).
//
// The second is ADMISSIBILITY TO A SERVING SUBJECT: which units a particular deployment may use. A
// host is admissible to subject S iff EVERY commitment cause on it is held by S. That reading is
// what lets the pair unit on group A see its own four hosts as available while everyone else sees
// them as taken, and it is where a DECLARED cause enters: the operator's ruling that group B is
// GLM's reserves srv9-srv12 for the canary's subject for SERVING, and for serving only. Its
// projection is `spark_serving_admissible_hosts`, consumed by gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection
// as the candidate domain and the fit. The scope difference is deliberate and is argued on the
// reservation row itself: a ruling about which model a cage serves says nothing about where a
// container may be built, so widening it would refuse the build lanes for a reason the ruling never
// gave.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE, STATED SO IT IS READ AS INTENDED AND NOT AS A DEFECT: with group B reserved, no
// unit is free of every cause, so `SparkHostUncommitted` has no production inhabitant today while
// `spark_unplaced_hosts` still answers srv10-srv12. The witness asserts both BY IDENTITY, because an
// empty set is an absorbing value and "nothing is free" must be shown rather than assumed.

// A COMMITMENT IS NOT A CAPABILITY. Nothing here says a host cannot serve or cannot build. It says
// some other authority has already placed work on it or reserved it, so a lane that places NEW work
// must not treat it as free. The remedy is always the same and always elsewhere: release the
// reservation, withdraw the group, change the role, or record the ruling that supersedes the one a
// reservation cites. Every one of those widens this set with no edit to this file, which is the
// property that makes the derivation worth having over a list.
//
// WHY A CELL ROLE COMMITS, INCLUDING A SERVING ROLE. The obvious reading of an exclusion list is
// "hosts that cannot serve", and under that reading a SparkServingCell excluding itself is absurd.
// That is the wrong reading -- the question is which hosts are UNCOMMITTED. gunbc.spark.cell_role
// gives the physical reason in its own note: two roles on one unified memory pool have no arbiter.
// Placing a freshly selected deployment beside an already-converged one is that same contention
// arriving through a different door, and the remedy cell_role states for the training case is the
// general one -- a deployment that wants an assigned cell must CHANGE THE ASSIGNMENT, not run
// beside it.
//
// WHY AN UNDECIDABLE ROLE ALSO COMMITS. `spark_cell_role_in` answers a third arm: two rows naming one
// host, which no row's closed sum can prevent because the ambiguity is in the ROSTER rather than in
// any row. A lookup that cannot say what a host is committed to has not said the host is free, and
// reading it as free is the absence-as-fact inference this fleet's authorities keep refusing.
//
// THE SERVING-GROUP ARM IS THE ACTIVE GROUPS, NOT EVERY CAGE, and that distinction is inherited
// rather than re-reasoned: a FabricGroup is a 400G cage of four hosts, so subtracting every group
// subtracts the whole fleet. A cage is where a group COULD serve; spark_pair_serving_groups is where
// one DOES. The build lane learned that from a red witness and the reasoning is its, not this file's.
//
// THE SERVING-ARM CLAIM IS THE CAUSE THIS CENSUS SHIPPED WITHOUT, AND IT WAS A FAIL-OPEN.
//
// The first cut read cell roles, claimed pair-serving groups and held llama.cpp reservations, and
// declared srv9 uncommitted. srv9 is where the GLM canary is serving. gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment
// says so directly -- the canary is serving on srv9 and nothing in this repository launched it through
// the pair route -- and derives that route's head from the arm rather than from the pair roster,
// precisely because the pair claim on group B was withdrawn while the canary was not. An observer
// reading only the pair roster therefore sees srv9 as free and is wrong, and this module was that
// observer while claiming to be the authority on what every unit is spoken for by.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE WAS NOT ACADEMIC, which is why the cause is added rather than the note softened.
// Both consumers of this set authorise effects on the host they are handed: a source build that
// occupies a machine for most of an hour, and an image probe that pulls a 9.6 GB image and starts a
// container. Either, pointed at srv9, would have landed on a machine answering live requests.
//
// THE CLAIM IS READ FROM BELOW, NOT FROM THE ARM. gunbc.spark.serving_arm_placement carries the
// placement fact on its own, because the canary's convergence root imports serving selection, and
// serving selection imports THIS module -- reaching up into the canary module would close that loop.
// The placement authority also carries why release is a receipt rather than an edit, which is the
// same lifecycle the reservation arm below already enforces.
//
// THE HELD RESERVATION IS A WALL THAT IS CURRENTLY QUIET, NOT A DEAD ONE.
// `llama_cpp_rpc_undischarged_peer_reservation_hosts()` is EMPTY on this corpus today: the one minted
// internal-participant reservation (srv8, the weights peer) was cleared by a release receipt on
// 2026-09-05, as was srv7's, and test.claim.spark.spark_llama_cpp_rpc_witness asserts exactly that.
// So this arm commits nobody at the moment -- srv7 and srv8 are held by the serving group instead --
// and that is the reservation lifecycle working rather than an arm with nothing behind it: a positive
// observation mints, only a positive receipt clears, and not looking again is not a release. Its
// evidence is therefore taken over FIXTURE rosters, which is the same argument `spark_cell_role_in`
// makes for existing beside `spark_cell_role_of`: a capability's evidence must not depend on which
// machines currently hold what.

// A COMMITMENT IS A CAUSE, NOT A BOOLEAN, AND A HOST MAY HOLD MORE THAN ONE.
//
// A `Bool` here would answer the screen and lose the report, and a single-cause sum would force a
// precedence between facts that are independently true -- a host can be a serving-group member AND
// hold a reservation, and dropping either makes a later release receipt look like it freed a host it
// did not free.
//
// AUTHORITY-HELD AND RULING-DERIVED CAUSES ARE SPLIT AT THE TYPE LEVEL, NOT BY NAME.
//
// THE LINE IS NAMED FOR WHAT IT IS, AND IT IS NOT "OBSERVED VERSUS DECLARED". A first cut called the
// outer arms Observed and Declared, and the parent lane caught that the name was false for half the
// members: a claimed serving group is gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired's DESIRED state, and a cell
// role is an operator decision recorded in gunbc.spark.cell_role -- neither is an observation of the
// host. Only the RPC reservation is observation-minted, and the arm placement is an authored row.
// What the four actually share is that the AUTHORITY OWNING THE FACT HOLDS A STANDING ON THE HOST --
// a role convergence actuates, a claim convergence acts on, an arm that answers, a reservation a
// receipt discharges -- and a fleet-side event ends each of them: change the role, withdraw the
// group, stop the arm, land the release receipt.
//
// A RULING-DERIVED cause is different in kind: nothing on the fleet holds it. It exists because a
// person ruled, and it ends only when the ruling is superseded, which the fleet will never signal.
// If both arrived as arms of one sum, a reader could not ask "which of this host's causes does an
// authority hold on it, and which exist only by ruling?" without knowing the arm names, and the
// ruling would be read as the kind something is doing on the host -- policy dressed as a standing,
// the meaning fork DESIGN section 3 names. So the outer sum carries the kind,
// `spark_authority_held_causes_of` and `spark_ruling_derived_causes_of` answer that question by
// matching it, and the wire renders the kind too.
type SparkHostAuthorityCommitment
  = CommittedToCellRole { role: SparkCellRole }
  | MemberOfClaimedServingGroup { group: FabricGroup }
  | OccupiedByServingArm { claim: ServingArmHostClaim }
  | HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation
  | OccupiedByHostEffect { purpose: NonEmptyStr, executor: NonEmptyStr }
  | CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment> }

// THE ONE RULING-DERIVED CAUSE, AND ITS BASIS IS A CITATION RATHER THAN A SENTENCE. gunbc.spark.serving_subject
// carries the ruling and the row that turns it into hosts; this arm records, per host, which subject
// the ruling reserved it for and cites the ruling so a reader can ask whether it still stands.
type SparkHostRulingCommitment
  = ReservedForServingSubject { subject: ServingSubject, basis: ServingRulingCitation }

type SparkHostCommitment
  = AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: SparkHostAuthorityCommitment }
  | RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: SparkHostRulingCommitment }

// A CLAIMED GROUP MEMBER IS A JOIN ROW, so the cause can carry the group it came from. The production
// application derives these from the claimed groups through observed cage membership; a fixture can
// name them directly, so the evidence for the group arm does not depend on which cage a unit is
// cabled into today.
type ClaimedServingGroupMember {
  group: FabricGroup
  host: HostIdentity
}

// UNCOMMITTED AND COMMITTED ARE DECIDED BY THE SAME DESTRUCTURE THAT SUPPLIES THE PAYLOAD.
//
// `SparkHostCommitted { commitments: [] }` -- a host spoken for by nothing -- is the state that must
// not be writable, and the way it is made unwritable is std.algebra's free semigroup, exactly as
// std.goal_assessment mints a divergence: the cause list is MATCHED, and the committed arm holds a
// head it was handed. An `if length(causes) == 0` fork would leave the empty commitment one edit away
// from being written.
type SparkHostStanding
  = SparkHostUncommitted { host: HostIdentity }
  | SparkHostCommitted { host: HostIdentity, commitments: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment> }

// The semigroup flattened for traversal, the way gunbc.change_realization reads its staged steps.
fn spark_commitment_causes(c: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>) -> List<SparkHostCommitment> {
  concat([c.head], c.tail)
}

fn spark_host_commitments_in(
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  claimed_group_members: List<ClaimedServingGroupMember>,
  arm_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim>,
  held_reservation_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  serving_reservations: List<SparkServingReservation>,
  host_effects: List<HostEffectRecord>,
  host: HostIdentity,
) -> FreeMonoid<SparkHostCommitment> {
  let ruled =
    fold(
      filter(serving_reservations, r => any(r.hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host))),
      init: Empty {},
      f: fn(acc, r) {
        Cons { head: RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: ReservedForServingSubject { subject: r.subject, basis: r.basis } }, tail: acc }
      },
    )
  let effected =
    fold(
      filter(host_effects, r => !r.released && host_identity_eq(a: r.host, b: host)),
      init: ruled,
      f: fn(acc, r) { Cons { head: AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: OccupiedByHostEffect { purpose: r.purpose, executor: r.executor } }, tail: acc } },
    )
  let reserved =
    if any(held_reservation_hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)) {
      Cons { head: AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation }, tail: effected }
    } else {
      effected
    }
  let armed =
    fold(
      filter(arm_claims, c => host_identity_eq(a: c.host, b: host)),
      init: reserved,
      f: fn(acc, c) { Cons { head: AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: OccupiedByServingArm { claim: c } }, tail: acc } },
    )
  let serving =
    fold(
      filter(claimed_group_members, m => host_identity_eq(a: m.host, b: host)),
      init: armed,
      f: fn(acc, m) { Cons { head: AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: MemberOfClaimedServingGroup { group: m.group } }, tail: acc } },
    )
  match spark_cell_role_in(assignments: assignments, host: host) {
    SparkCellRoleAbsent => serving
    SparkCellRoleUnique { role: r } =>
      Cons { head: AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: CommittedToCellRole { role: r } }, tail: serving }
    SparkCellRoleAmbiguous { assignments: rows } =>
      Cons { head: AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: rows } }, tail: serving }
  }
}

// A STANDING HAS EXACTLY ONE CONSTRUCTION SITE, AND IT IS THE TRAVERSAL OF THE POPULATION ITSELF.
//
// THIS IS THE THIRD CUT AT ONE DEFECT AND THE PREVIOUS TWO EXPLAIN THE SHAPE. The original producer
// took a host and four commitment lists and answered a STANDING; handed a machine that is not a DGX
// Spark unit it found no cause and said `SparkHostUncommitted`, which reads as "the fleet looked and
// nothing has claimed it" when the fleet had not looked. The first repair added the population and an
// Absent arm but moved the destructure into a `spark_standing_from_causes(host, causes)` helper --
// the same unrestricted mint one level down, because naming a helper "the mint" does not give it
// module privacy: `.dag` has no visibility modifier, so an unchecked constructor is a public one. The
// second repair deleted that helper and inlined the destructure behind a per-host membership scan.
//
// THAT SECOND REPAIR WAS CORRECT AND ITS ANNOTATION WAS WRONG, which is the part worth keeping. The
// note here offered a choice between a repeated membership scan and the return of an unchecked mint,
// and called the resulting quadratic the necessary price of one construction authority. It is not.
// DESIGN section 6 says a proven cost-shape defect is ALWAYS fixed regardless of the realized n, and
// names "n is small here" as not a time-stable justification -- so an eight-row roster does not
// discharge it, and pricing a per-site exception is the move that rule exists to refuse. The
// arithmetic was real: with n hosts and every commitment list empty, the outer traversal asked the
// inner producer to rediscover each host in the same roster, so membership work grew as n(n+1)/2 with
// no causes to join at all.
//
// THE DISTINCTION THE FALSE CHOICE MISSED: A CONSTRUCTOR THAT TAKES A POPULATION AND CONSTRUCTS ONLY
// WHILE TRAVERSING THAT POPULATION IS ALREADY POPULATION-BOUNDED. There is no independent host input
// at the construction site to check, because the host being constructed for CAME FROM the traversal
// -- membership follows from where it came from rather than from a scan asking whether it is there.
// That is not the deleted function, which accepted an arbitrary host with no population in sight. So
// the fold below is the sole construction authority, the destructure sits directly inside its walk of
// `units`, and the per-host answer is a LOOKUP over the constructed result rather than a second mint.
// One authority, no rescan, no proof type, and nothing for a later edit to route around.
//
// `spark_host_commitments_in` stays public and stays unchecked ON PURPOSE: it answers which of the
// lists you handed it name this host -- a question about the lists, not a claim about the fleet --
// and it returns a cause monoid rather than a standing, so it is not another standing-producing
// boundary and keeping it public does not recreate the deleted mint.

fn spark_host_standings_in(
  units: List<HostIdentity>,
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  claimed_group_members: List<ClaimedServingGroupMember>,
  arm_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim>,
  held_reservation_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  serving_reservations: List<SparkServingReservation>,
  host_effects: List<HostEffectRecord>,
) -> List<SparkHostStanding> {
  map(units, h => match spark_host_commitments_in(
    assignments: assignments,
    claimed_group_members: claimed_group_members,
    arm_claims: arm_claims,
    held_reservation_hosts: held_reservation_hosts,
    serving_reservations: serving_reservations,
    host_effects: host_effects,
    host: h,
  ) {
    Empty => SparkHostUncommitted { host: h }
    Cons { head: first, tail: rest } =>
      SparkHostCommitted { host: h, commitments: FreeSemigroup { head: first, tail: rest } }
  })
}

// THE PER-HOST ANSWER IS A LOOKUP OVER THAT RESULT, NOT A SECOND MINT. It constructs nothing: it asks
// the constructed population whether it holds the host, so an outside-population host has no standing
// to find and the Absent arm is a consequence of the roster rather than a check standing beside it.
fn spark_host_standing_in(
  units: List<HostIdentity>,
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  claimed_group_members: List<ClaimedServingGroupMember>,
  arm_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim>,
  held_reservation_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  serving_reservations: List<SparkServingReservation>,
  host_effects: List<HostEffectRecord>,
  host: HostIdentity,
) -> SparkHostStanding? {
  spark_standing_of(
    standings: spark_host_standings_in(
      units: units,
      assignments: assignments,
      claimed_group_members: claimed_group_members,
      arm_claims: arm_claims,
      held_reservation_hosts: held_reservation_hosts,
      serving_reservations: serving_reservations,
      host_effects: host_effects,
    ),
    host: host,
  )
}

// ── THE NEW-PLACEMENT PROJECTION: AUTHORITY-HELD CAUSES DECIDE, RULING-DERIVED ONES DO NOT ─────
//
// THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES HAD TWO ROOTS. `spark_host_standings` folded the commitment sources, and
// `spark_uncommitted_hosts` folded the same sources again to answer the narrower question -- so the
// module whose whole purpose is to stop two derivations of one fact from drifting contained two
// derivations of one fact. These take a standings list and read it; there is nowhere in them for an
// input to be forgotten, because they have no inputs to forget.
//
// AND THE NAME CHANGED WITH THE QUESTION. `spark_uncommitted_hosts` answered "no cause at all", which
// coincided with "nothing has placed work here" only while every cause was held by an authority on
// the host. Now that a ruling can reserve a host for serving without anything standing on it, the
// two questions have different answers on srv10-srv12, and one name for both would be the meaning
// fork section 3 forbids. "Unplaced" is the placement question: no AUTHORITY-HELD cause.
// `SparkHostUncommitted` keeps the stricter meaning -- no cause of any kind -- and is what the
// serving-admissibility fold reads.
fn spark_authority_held_causes_of(c: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>) -> List<SparkHostAuthorityCommitment> {
  flat_map(spark_commitment_causes(c: c), x => match x {
    AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: o } => [o]
    RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: _ } => []
  })
}

fn spark_ruling_derived_causes_of(c: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>) -> List<SparkHostRulingCommitment> {
  flat_map(spark_commitment_causes(c: c), x => match x {
    AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: _ } => []
    RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: d } => [d]
  })
}

fn spark_unplaced_hosts_of(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  flat_map(standings, s => match s {
    SparkHostUncommitted { host: h } => [h]
    SparkHostCommitted { host: h, commitments: c } =>
      if length(spark_authority_held_causes_of(c: c)) == 0 { [h] } else { [] }
  })
}

fn spark_committed_standings_of(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>) -> List<SparkHostStanding> {
  filter(standings, s => match s {
    SparkHostUncommitted { host: _ } => false
    SparkHostCommitted { host: _, commitments: _ } => true
  })
}

// The lookup folds the matched rows into an optional exactly as gunbc.spark.cell_role's own lookup
// does, rather than reaching for a list head this substrate does not expose as a free function.
fn spark_standing_of(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>, host: HostIdentity) -> SparkHostStanding? {
  fold(
    filter(standings, s => match s {
      SparkHostUncommitted { host: h } => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)
      SparkHostCommitted { host: h, commitments: _ } => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)
    }),
    init: none,
    f: fn(acc, s) { Present { value: s } },
  )
}

// ── ADMISSIBILITY TO A SERVING SUBJECT: EVERY CAUSE ON THE HOST MUST BE HELD BY THAT SUBJECT ──
//
// WHO HOLDS WHICH CAUSE is a relation over the cause, the subject and the host's OTHER causes, and
// each arm is a stated rule rather than a default:
//   - a claimed group's membership is held by the pair unit on that group and by nobody else;
//   - a placed arm's occupancy is held by that arm and by nobody else;
//   - a ruling's reservation is held by the subject it names;
//   - a SERVING cell role is held by whichever serving subject claims the host -- through a group
//     membership, an arm placement or a reservation on the same host -- because the role says the
//     cell is converged toward serving and the subject claiming it is what it is converged for; a
//     serving role on a host no subject claims is held by nobody;
//   - a TRAINING role, an undischarged RPC reservation and an undecidable role are held by NOBODY:
//     none of them is a serving deployment, so no serving subject can count such a host.
// The last arm is the one the scalar could never express, and it is why "held by nobody" is a
// refusal for every subject rather than an absence that reads as freedom.
fn serving_subject_claims_host(subject: ServingSubject, siblings: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>) -> Bool {
  any(spark_commitment_causes(c: siblings), c => match c {
    AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: o } =>
      match o {
        MemberOfClaimedServingGroup { group: g } => serving_subject_eq(a: subject, b: PairServingUnitOn { group: g })
        OccupiedByServingArm { claim: cl } => serving_subject_eq(a: subject, b: PlacedServingArm { arm: cl.arm })
        CommittedToCellRole { role: _ } => false
        HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation => false
        OccupiedByHostEffect { purpose: _, executor: _ } => false
        CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: _ } => false
      }
    RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: d } =>
      match d {
        ReservedForServingSubject { subject: s, basis: _ } => serving_subject_eq(a: subject, b: s)
      }
  })
}

fn spark_commitment_held_by(c: SparkHostCommitment, siblings: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>, subject: ServingSubject) -> Bool {
  match c {
    AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: o } =>
      match o {
        CommittedToCellRole { role: r } =>
          match r {
            SparkServingCell => serving_subject_claims_host(subject: subject, siblings: siblings)
            SparkTrainingCell => false
          }
        MemberOfClaimedServingGroup { group: g } => serving_subject_eq(a: subject, b: PairServingUnitOn { group: g })
        OccupiedByServingArm { claim: cl } => serving_subject_eq(a: subject, b: PlacedServingArm { arm: cl.arm })
        HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation => false
        OccupiedByHostEffect { purpose: _, executor: _ } => false
        CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: _ } => false
      }
    RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: d } =>
      match d {
        ReservedForServingSubject { subject: s, basis: _ } => serving_subject_eq(a: subject, b: s)
      }
  }
}

// THE PER-HOST ANSWER CARRIES THE CAUSES THAT REFUSED, AND THE REFUSING ARM IS MINTED BY THE SAME
// DESTRUCTURE THAT DECIDES IT -- the free-semigroup idiom again, so "held against S by nothing" has
// no constructor.
type SparkHostAdmissibility
  = SparkHostAdmissibleTo { host: HostIdentity, subject: ServingSubject }
  | SparkHostHeldAgainst { host: HostIdentity, subject: ServingSubject, unheld: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment> }

fn spark_host_admissibility_to(standing: SparkHostStanding, subject: ServingSubject) -> SparkHostAdmissibility {
  match standing {
    SparkHostUncommitted { host: h } => SparkHostAdmissibleTo { host: h, subject: subject }
    SparkHostCommitted { host: h, commitments: c } =>
      match fold(
        spark_commitment_causes(c: c),
        init: Empty {},
        f: fn(acc, cause) {
          if spark_commitment_held_by(c: cause, siblings: c, subject: subject) { acc } else { Cons { head: cause, tail: acc } }
        },
      ) {
        Empty => SparkHostAdmissibleTo { host: h, subject: subject }
        Cons { head: first, tail: rest } =>
          SparkHostHeldAgainst { host: h, subject: subject, unheld: FreeSemigroup { head: first, tail: rest } }
      }
  }
}

fn spark_admissibility_to(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>, subject: ServingSubject) -> List<SparkHostAdmissibility> {
  map(standings, s => spark_host_admissibility_to(standing: s, subject: subject))
}

fn spark_admissible_hosts_of(admissibility: List<SparkHostAdmissibility>) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  flat_map(admissibility, a => match a {
    SparkHostAdmissibleTo { host: h, subject: _ } => [h]
    SparkHostHeldAgainst { host: _, subject: _, unheld: _ } => []
  })
}

fn spark_admissible_hosts_to(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>, subject: ServingSubject) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  spark_admissible_hosts_of(admissibility: spark_admissibility_to(standings: standings, subject: subject))
}

// THE PRODUCTION APPLICATION, AND IT IS THE ONLY PLACE THE COMMITMENT SOURCES ARE NAMED TOGETHER.
// Everything below is a projection of this one value.
//
// IT TAKES THE AUTHORITIES IT FILTERS, so a control can supply a released one and watch four hosts
// leave. Every live row commits hosts today, so a claim over the live fold alone cannot tell the filter
// from its absence -- deleting the filter would keep every count identical. Supplying the population is
// what makes the filter's removal observable.
//
// IT READS AUTHORITY, NOT MEMBERSHIP, AND THE DIFFERENCE ONLY SHOWS UNDER A TRANSACTION. Claiming a
// group and being free to act on it are two facts; this fold wants the first, and mapping the roster
// directly conflated them. Today every claimed group is PairServingActive, so the population is
// identical and this change moves no host. What it buys is the suspended case: a group mid-replacement
// still commits its four hosts, so nothing reads the pause as free capacity and lands a build or a
// second lane on a host we are holding. gunbc.spark.pair_serving_authority pair_serving_commits_hosts
// is where that answer lives, and ReleasedToFleet is the only arm that drops the commitment.
// WHICH hosts is the authority's own answer (pair_serving_committed_hosts): a transaction's states
// carry the exact population they were authorized over, so a roster that moves mid-transaction
// changes nothing here until the transaction ends.
fn spark_claimed_members_under(authorities: List<PairServingGroupAuthority>) -> List<ClaimedServingGroupMember> {
  flat_map(
    filter(authorities, a => pair_serving_commits_hosts(a: a)),
    a => {
      let g = pair_serving_authority_group(a: a)
      map(pair_serving_committed_hosts(a: a), h => ClaimedServingGroupMember { group: g, host: h })
    })
}

fn spark_claimed_serving_group_members() -> List<ClaimedServingGroupMember> {
  spark_claimed_members_under(authorities: spark_pair_serving_authorities)
}

fn spark_claimed_serving_group_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  map(spark_claimed_serving_group_members(), m => m.host)
}

// THE STANDINGS OVER SUPPLIED AUTHORITIES. The resting projection below hands in the source rows
// (pure, the population every pure consumer reads); the effectful admission seams hand in the
// CURRENT authorities read from the event log (spark_host_standings_current), so an effect that
// runs while a transaction is open commits the hosts the transaction froze, not the roster.
fn spark_host_standings_under(authorities: List<PairServingGroupAuthority>, host_effects: List<HostEffectRecord>) -> List<SparkHostStanding> {
  spark_host_standings_in(
    units: dgx_spark_reserved_identities(),
    assignments: spark_cell_role_assignments,
    claimed_group_members: spark_claimed_members_under(authorities: authorities),
    arm_claims: spark_serving_arm_host_claims,
    held_reservation_hosts: llama_cpp_rpc_undischarged_peer_reservation_hosts(),
    serving_reservations: spark_serving_reservations,
    host_effects: host_effects,
  )
}

data spark_host_standings: List<SparkHostStanding> = spark_host_standings_under(authorities: spark_pair_serving_authorities, host_effects: [] as List<HostEffectRecord>)

data spark_unplaced_hosts: List<HostIdentity> = spark_unplaced_hosts_of(standings: spark_host_standings)

// ── THE CURRENT STANDINGS, FOR AN EFFECT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// An effect that places work on a Spark host (a build, a probe, a source acquisition) may run
// while a pair-serving transaction is open, and then the resting row is not the authority: the
// event log is (gunbc.spark.pair_serving_authority_log). So the admission an effect asks is over
// the CURRENT authorities of every claimed group, read on the executor's store, and refuses when
// any of them could not be read -- an unread authority is not an empty commitment.
type CurrentStandings
  = CurrentStandingsRead { standings: List<SparkHostStanding> }
  | CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE LIVE HOST EFFECTS ARE COMMITMENTS TOO: they are supplied by the caller, who read the one
// placement partition (spark_host_standings_over). An effect that is live -- unreleased, however
// old -- commits its host until its release lands with quiescence evidence.
fn spark_host_standings_current(current: List<CurrentAuthority>, host_claims: List<HostEffectRecord>) -> CurrentStandings {
  let unread = flat_map(current, c => match c {
    CurrentAuthorityUnread { group: g, step: st, reason: why } => [join([fabric_group_wire(g: g) as String, " at ", st, ": ", why], "")]
    CurrentAuthorityRead { authority: _ } => [] as List<String>
  })
  if length(unread) != 0 {
    CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: join(["the current pair-serving authority could not be read for every claimed group, so host commitment cannot be derived: ", join(unread, "; ")], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    CurrentStandingsRead { standings: spark_host_standings_under(authorities: flat_map(current, c => match c { CurrentAuthorityRead { authority: a } => [a]  CurrentAuthorityUnread { group: _, step: _, reason: _ } => [] as List<PairServingGroupAuthority> }), host_effects: host_claims) }
  }
}

// THE CURRENT STANDINGS OVER THE LOG. Each group partition is read by the one authority fold from
// its durable genesis: an established group contributes its current authority; a group that is
// unestablished AND undeclared in the source roster contributes nothing; a group the source
// roster declares but the log has not established is UNREAD -- the desired row has not been
// actuated (pair_serving_authority_establish_wet), and until it is, nothing may treat its hosts
// as free. The live host effects come from the one placement partition. Any unread partition is
// the whole reading unread.
type GroupStanding
  = GroupStandingEstablished { current: CurrentAuthority }
  | GroupStandingUnclaimed
  | GroupStandingUnread { cause: String }

fn group_standing_over(group: FabricGroup, partition: GroupPartitionRead, placement: PlacementRead, at: EpochSecs) -> GroupStanding {
  match partition {
    GroupPartitionUnread { step: st, reason: why } => GroupStandingUnread { cause: join([fabric_group_wire(g: group) as String, " at ", st, ": ", why], "") }
    GroupPartitionAt { head: _, fold: f } =>
      match f {
        AuthorityFoldRefused { at_event: e, reason: why } => GroupStandingUnread { cause: join([fabric_group_wire(g: group) as String, ": ", fold_refusal_text(at_event: e, reason: why)], "") }
        AuthorityUnestablished { cancelled: _ } =>
          match pair_serving_authority_for(group: group) {
            Absent => GroupStandingUnclaimed
            Present { value: _ } => GroupStandingUnread { cause: join([fabric_group_wire(g: group) as String, ": the source roster declares an authority that has not been established on the log (pair_serving_authority_establish_wet)"], "") }
          }
        AuthorityFolded { current: _ } => GroupStandingEstablished { current: current_authority_over(group: group, partition: partition, placement: placement, at: at) }
      }
  }
}

// ONE READ PER PARTITION: the placement fold and each group's partition are read once, and every
// standing and the joined placement are decided over that one snapshot -- never over independent
// re-reads of a live store that could disagree with each other.
fn spark_host_standings_over(store: FabricStorageBinding, at: EpochSecs) -> CurrentStandings {
  let placement = placement_fold_read(store: store)
  let groups = map([FabricGroupA, FabricGroupB], g => GroupSnapshot { group: g, partition: read_group_partition(store: store, group: g) })
  let standings = map(groups, gs => group_standing_over(group: gs.group, partition: gs.partition, placement: placement, at: at))
  let unread = flat_map(standings, st => match st { GroupStandingUnread { cause: c } => [c]  _ => [] as List<String> })
  if length(unread) != 0 {
    CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: join(["a group partition could not be read, so host commitment cannot be derived: ", join(unread, "; ")], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    let current = flat_map(standings, st => match st { GroupStandingEstablished { current: c } => [c]  _ => [] as List<CurrentAuthority> })
    match placement_read_over(placement: placement, groups: groups) {
      PlacementUnread { step: st, reason: why } => CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: join(["the host-placement partition could not be read at ", st, ", so host commitment cannot be derived: ", why], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      PlacementReadAt { head: _, effects: effs, preparations: _ } => spark_host_standings_current(current: current, host_claims: effs)
    }
  }
}

fn spark_host_standings_live() -> CurrentStandings {
  match observe_executor_reach() {
    ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: join(["the executor could not be identified, so the current authority cannot be read: ", c], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: executor, path: _ } =>
      match now_epoch_seconds() {
        Absent => CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: "the clock could not be read as epoch seconds, so the lease standing of the current authority cannot be derived" as NonEmptyStr }
        Present { value: at } =>
          match event_log_store_for_host(short_hostname: executor) {
            HostStoreRefused { detail: d } => CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: d as NonEmptyStr }
            HostStoreResolved { store: store, executor: _ } => spark_host_standings_over(store: store, at: at)
          }
      }
  }
}

fn spark_host_is_unplaced(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  any(spark_unplaced_hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host))
}

fn spark_host_standing(host: HostIdentity) -> SparkHostStanding? {
  spark_standing_of(standings: spark_host_standings, host: host)
}

fn spark_serving_admissibility(subject: ServingSubject) -> List<SparkHostAdmissibility> {
  spark_admissibility_to(standings: spark_host_standings, subject: subject)
}

fn spark_serving_admissible_hosts(subject: ServingSubject) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  spark_admissible_hosts_to(standings: spark_host_standings, subject: subject)
}

// ADMISSION BY THE SUBJECT THAT HOLDS THE HOST, for an effect that stages bytes at rest on a committed
// host on its owner's behalf (no process, container, seat or live allocation). The host must be one the
// named subject holds; any other refuses with the held list rendered. The decision takes the held set
// as a supplied value so a claim can drive it; the live seam reads it from spark_serving_admissible_hosts.
// It lands no host-effect claim -- a committed-host staging claim carried on the owner's behalf would be
// the stronger construction, and is not modeled here yet.
fn admit_host_held_by_subject_among(raw: HostIdentity, subject: ServingSubject, held: List<HostIdentity>) -> String? {
  if any(held, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: raw)) { none } else {
    Present { value: join([raw as String, " is not held by ", serving_subject_wire(s: subject) as String, "; this effect runs only on hosts that subject holds, which are: ", join(map(held, h => h as String), ", ")], "") }
  }
}

fn admit_host_held_by_subject(raw: HostIdentity, subject: ServingSubject) -> String? {
  admit_host_held_by_subject_among(raw: raw, subject: subject, held: spark_serving_admissible_hosts(subject: subject))
}

// WHY A HOST IS NOT AVAILABLE, RENDERED FROM THE STANDING ITSELF.
//
// THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES WAS A HAND-MAINTAINED SENTENCE. `spark_uncommitted_hosts_how` listed the
// producers in prose and claimed it could not go stale because it named them rather than transcribing
// their answer. That was half true and the wrong half was load-bearing: it was still a second list of
// the commitment sources, maintained by hand, and adding the serving-arm cause did not update it --
// measured, because adding that cause is exactly what happened. A refusal now renders the CAUSES THIS
// HOST ACTUALLY CARRIES, which no edit can desynchronise from the fold, and which tells an operator
// the one thing the generic sentence never did: which authority to go and talk to.
// ONE PASS AND ONE JOIN, BECAUSE THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES RE-COPIED ITS OWN SUFFIX.
//
// `spark_commitment_tail_wire` recursed down the tail and joined the head's rendering to the ENTIRE
// rendered remainder at every step, so each level copied the whole suffix again -- quadratic in the
// number of causes. DESIGN section 6 rules that a proven cost-shape defect is always fixed regardless
// of the realized n, and it is right to insist even where a host carries few causes: n is not a
// time-stable fact, and pricing a per-site exception is itself redundant work.
//
// THE OPERATIONS ARE INHABITED, AND THE SYMBOLS THAT SAY SO ARE NAMED HERE RATHER THAN DESCRIBED.
// `std.algebra` `algebra_carriers` carries an `AlgebraCarrier` row whose name is "FreeMonoid" with
// profile `FreeMonoidCollectionProfile`, and `std.algebra` `free_monoid_collection_templates`
// declares `map` and `join` among that profile's operations. So no local rendering fold is authored:
// the causes are mapped once and joined once through operations the carrier already has.
//
// THAT SENTENCE USED TO SAY "FreeMonoid inhabits the collection profile", AND THE REWRITE IS THE
// LESSON. The earlier annotation did not name the declarations supporting its claim, so a reviewer
// checking it had only an explanatory phrase to search for, found that phrase nowhere but in the
// annotation itself, and reported the method surface as resolving nowhere in the tree (review 64412).
// A failed search for an explanatory phrase did not establish that the operations are absent -- an
// unestablished claim and an established false one are different evidentiary states, and collapsing
// them would license treating any failed grep as a disproof. What the episode does establish is the
// duty: DESIGN section 3 asks for the symbol, the symbol citations above locate the declared
// operations, and resolution and execution require their own evidence beyond any citation.
//
// AND THE ANNOTATION IS NOT THAT EVIDENCE, which section 4c settles: no Accepted program can read one.
// The executing evidence is
// `test.claim.spark.spark_host_commitment_witness` `a_multi_cause_host_renders_every_cause_with_both_separators`,
// which renders a host committed by a cell role AND a claimed serving group AND a held reservation at
// once and compares the result to a written expectation carrying both separators.
//
// WHY THAT WITNESS EXISTS, stated exactly, because an earlier version of this note got the coverage
// history wrong. It is NOT that every other assertion here exercised a single cause --
// `a_committed_host_carries_every_cause_that_commits_it` already constructs and inspects a three-cause
// payload, and the admission witness already reads a refusal text. The distinction is between PAYLOAD
// COVERAGE and JOINT RENDERING: those assertions did not require the complete three-cause rendering
// and both intervening separators to survive together, and this one exercises that composition
// through the production renderer. The general form of the error is worth keeping -- an assertion's
// SCOPE and the PATH IT EXECUTES are different things, and checking one fragment of an output does
// not establish that only one input was processed.
fn spark_commitment_causes_text(c: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>) -> String {
  let every_cause = Cons { head: c.head, tail: c.tail }
  every_cause.map(x => spark_host_commitment_wire(c: x) as String).join("; ")
}

fn spark_host_commitment_causes_wire(host: HostIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match spark_host_standing(host: host) {
    Absent => "that host is not a DGX Spark unit in gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement, so this fleet has no standing for it" as NonEmptyStr
    Present { value: standing } =>
      match standing {
        SparkHostUncommitted { host: _ } => "nothing has claimed that host" as NonEmptyStr
        SparkHostCommitted { host: _, commitments: c } => spark_commitment_causes_text(c: c) as NonEmptyStr
      }
  }
}

// THE WIRE SAYS WHICH KIND A CAUSE IS. An authority-held cause reads as the standing it is; a
// ruling-derived one reads as "reserved by ruling", with the decider and date, so a refusal quoting
// it cannot be taken for something standing on the host.
fn spark_authority_commitment_wire(c: SparkHostAuthorityCommitment) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    CommittedToCellRole { role: _ } => "committed to a Spark cell role" as NonEmptyStr
    MemberOfClaimedServingGroup { group: g } => join(["a member of a serving group this repository claims (", fabric_group_wire(g: g) as String, ")"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    OccupiedByServingArm { claim: c2 } => join(["a serving arm is placed on it (", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: c2.arm), ")"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation => "holding an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation" as NonEmptyStr
    OccupiedByHostEffect { purpose: p, executor: x } => join(["held by a live host effect (", p as String, " by ", x as String, ") until it is released"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: _ } => "carrying more than one cell role row, so its commitment cannot be decided" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn spark_ruling_commitment_wire(c: SparkHostRulingCommitment) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    ReservedForServingSubject { subject: s, basis: b } =>
      join([
        "reserved by ruling for ", serving_subject_wire(s: s) as String,
        " (", b.decider as String, ", ", b.ruled_on as String, ": ", b.ruled as String, ")",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn spark_host_commitment_wire(c: SparkHostCommitment) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    AuthorityHeldCommitment { cause: o } => spark_authority_commitment_wire(c: o)
    RulingDerivedCommitment { cause: d } => spark_ruling_commitment_wire(c: d)
  }
}

// THE ONE ADMISSION SEAM, BECAUSE THREE EFFECTFUL ROOTS WERE EACH ASKING IN THEIR OWN WORDS.
//
// THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES: gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build owned a typed `admit_spark_build_host`
// that exactly one of its two entries called; the other entry, and the published-image probe in
// gunbc.spark.v41_runtime_image_probe, each re-wrote the guard as a bare `if !uncommitted` with their
// own refusal sentence. Three roots, one of them typed, and the witness exercised only the typed one
// -- so a regression in either open-coded guard left the suite green while an effect ran on a claimed
// host. The seam belongs to the authority that owns the commitment fact rather than to whichever lane
// needed it first, so it lives here and all three roots consume it.
//
// IT ANSWERS THE NEW-PLACEMENT QUESTION AND ONLY THAT ONE. A ruling reserving a cage for a serving
// subject does not refuse a build here, by the scope argument on gunbc.spark.serving_subject's
// reservation row; what refuses is an AUTHORITY-HELD cause, and the refusal renders the held causes
// the host carries so the operator is told which authority to talk to. A ruling-derived cause on the
// same host is not rendered into a refusal it did not decide.
//
// IT IS AN ELIGIBILITY ANSWER AND IT IS NOT A LEASE. THIS IS A STATED DIVERGENCE (DESIGN section 3b),
// not an oversight, and the honest form of it is the whole point. An unplaced host is an
// OBSERVATION over modelled persistent claims: it says nobody has written down a claim on this host.
// It is not a lease, not a compare-and-set, and it adds no claim of its own, so two runs can both
// read the same host as unplaced and both proceed. The generated fleet workflow does not close
// that gap either -- its concurrency key is `gunbc-host-mutation-${host}` over the EXECUTOR runner
// (srv1-srv4), while the Spark being mutated is a separate target input, so two runs dispatched
// through different executors can target one Spark without ever sharing a key.
//
// THE THREE-STEP SHAPE THIS OWES, stated so the gap is legible rather than papered over:
//   unplaced-set observation     -> candidate ELIGIBILITY only
//   target-host claim acquired   -> PERMISSION to mutate
//   terminal release receipt     -> host becomes claimable again
// This function answers the first and nothing else. THE SECOND AND THIRD NOW EXIST FOR AN EFFECT:
// admit_unplaced_host_over lands a host-effect claim on the fabric event log's host-placement
// partition against the head it read, so two concurrent runs against one
// target, dispatched through different executors, cannot both be admitted -- the second's claim
// is stale or held; and release_host_effect_live is the terminal that observes the host quiet and
// releases, which makes the host claimable again. This pure projection over the resting rows
// still holds no claim, and its name says so.
// AN ADMITTED HOST CARRIES THE CLAIM THAT FENCES IT. The admission is a claim event on the one
// host-placement partition (gunbc.spark.pair_serving_authority_log), appended against the head the
// admission read -- the same head every authority commitment is appended against, which is what
// makes it a fence and not a check. The handle carries what the release must observe: the host
// and the residue the effect declared. The resting projection admits with no claim (it places
// nothing).
type HostEffectHandle {
  host: HostIdentity
  residue: HostEffectResidueSpec
  claim: EventId
}

type SparkHostAdmission
  = SparkHostAdmitted { host: HostIdentity, claim: HostEffectHandle? }
  | SparkHostRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn spark_authority_held_causes_wire(host: HostIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr {
  spark_authority_held_causes_in(standings: spark_host_standings, host: host)
}

fn spark_authority_held_causes_in(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>, host: HostIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match spark_standing_of(standings: standings, host: host) {
    Absent => "that host is not a DGX Spark unit in gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement, so this fleet has no standing for it" as NonEmptyStr
    Present { value: standing } =>
      match standing {
        SparkHostUncommitted { host: _ } => "nothing has claimed that host" as NonEmptyStr
        SparkHostCommitted { host: _, commitments: c } =>
          join(map(spark_authority_held_causes_of(c: c), o => spark_authority_commitment_wire(c: o) as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr
      }
  }
}

// THE DECISION TAKES ITS POPULATION AS A SUPPLIED VALUE; THE PRODUCTION SEAM BELOW SUPPLIES THE LIVE ONE.
// Once every Spark unit carries a standing -- the state after group B's assigned arm claimed its
// four hosts on 2026-09-18 -- no production host is unplaced, so the admitting arm of this seam
// cannot be exercised against the live census at all. Supplying the unplaced set keeps that arm
// authorable as a positive control while every refusal arm still runs the real path through
// admit_unplaced_host (DESIGN section 3: a witness discriminates at one interface, and the real path
// keeps one inhabitance claim).
fn admit_unplaced_host_among(raw: String, purpose: NonEmptyStr, unplaced: List<HostIdentity>, held_causes: fn(HostIdentity) -> String) -> SparkHostAdmission {
  let host = raw as HostIdentity
  if raw == "" {
    SparkHostRefused { cause: join(["no host was named for ", purpose as String, "; this entry takes one and does not pick"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if !any(unplaced, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)) {
    SparkHostRefused {
      cause: join([
        raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ", because ",
        held_causes(host),
        ". Unplaced as of this read: ",
        join(map(unplaced, h => h as String), ", "),
        ". This is an eligibility read and not a held claim",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    SparkHostAdmitted { host: host, claim: none }
  }
}

// The same decision over a whole standings population: the unplaced set and the causes are both
// projections of it, so a caller with standings (the current-authority route) supplies one value.
fn admit_unplaced_host_in(raw: String, purpose: NonEmptyStr, standings: List<SparkHostStanding>) -> SparkHostAdmission {
  admit_unplaced_host_among(raw: raw, purpose: purpose, unplaced: spark_unplaced_hosts_of(standings: standings), held_causes: fn(h) { spark_authority_held_causes_in(standings: standings, host: h) as String })
}



// THE EFFECT'S ADMISSION IS A CLAIM, NOT A READ. The current standings admit, then the claim lands
// on the host-placement partition against the head that was read: stale when the partition moved
// in between, committed when a live authority commitment holds the host, held when another live
// effect has it. Every admitted host carries its claim; the effect releases it at its terminal
// with the observation that the host is quiet. `term` is the effect's own stated bound: past it
// the claim reads OVERDUE, and it still holds until released. `residue` is what the release must
// find absent -- the effect declares it here because only the effect knows what it leaves.
fn admit_unplaced_host_over(raw: String, purpose: NonEmptyStr, term: Second, residue: HostEffectResidueSpec, store: FabricStorageBinding, executor: NonEmptyStr, at: EpochSecs) -> SparkHostAdmission {
  match spark_host_standings_over(store: store, at: at) {
    CurrentStandingsUnread { cause: c } => SparkHostRefused { cause: join([raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ": ", c as String], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    CurrentStandingsRead { standings: st } =>
      match admit_unplaced_host_in(raw: raw, purpose: purpose, standings: st) {
        SparkHostRefused { cause: c } => SparkHostRefused { cause: c }
        SparkHostAdmitted { host: host, claim: _ } => {
          match host_effect_admit(store: store, host: host, purpose: purpose, executor: executor, term: term, residue: residue, at: at) {
            HostEffectClaimed { id: id } =>
              SparkHostAdmitted { host: host, claim: Present { value: HostEffectHandle { host: host, residue: residue, claim: id } } }
            HostEffectClaimRefused { partition: pt, step: st2, reason: why } => SparkHostRefused { cause: join([raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ": the host-effect claim did not land on ", pt as String, " at ", st2, ": ", why], "") as NonEmptyStr }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn admit_unplaced_host_live(raw: String, purpose: NonEmptyStr, term: Second, residue: HostEffectResidueSpec) -> SparkHostAdmission {
  match observe_executor_reach() {
    ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => SparkHostRefused { cause: join([raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ": the executor could not be identified: ", c], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: executor, path: _ } =>
      match event_log_store_for_host(short_hostname: executor) {
        HostStoreRefused { detail: d } => SparkHostRefused { cause: join([raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ": ", d], "") as NonEmptyStr }
        HostStoreResolved { store: store, executor: _ } =>
          match now_epoch_seconds() {
            Absent => SparkHostRefused { cause: join([raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ": the clock could not be read as epoch seconds"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
            Present { value: at } => admit_unplaced_host_over(raw: raw, purpose: purpose, term: term, residue: residue, store: store, executor: executor as NonEmptyStr, at: at)
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE RELEASE IS THE EVIDENCE LANDING: given a quiescence reading of the claim's host, append the
// release. A release that did not land is returned as its cause, never hidden -- the host stays
// held until one lands.
fn release_host_effect_over(handle: HostEffectHandle, evidence: HostQuiescenceEvidence, provenance: ReleaseProvenance, store: FabricStorageBinding, executor: NonEmptyStr, at: EpochSecs) -> String? {
  match host_effect_release(store: store, claim: handle.claim, evidence: evidence, provenance: provenance, executor: executor, at: at) {
    HostEffectReleasedAt { id: _ } => none
    HostEffectReleaseRefused { partition: pt, step: st, reason: why } => Present { value: join(["the host-effect claim ", handle.claim as String, " was not released on ", pt as String, " at ", st, ": ", why, "; the host stays held until a release with quiescence evidence lands"], "") }
  }
}

// THE OBSERVATION LEG TO A SPARK: the fleet automation principal over the runner's agent-held key,
// the same route the build seams take. A leg that could not be prepared, or that was refused
// before it ran, is a leg that did not run -- the observer reads that as UNREAD, never as quiet.
data host_effect_release_attempt_raw: String = "host-effect-release"

fn fleet_argv_run(context: FleetSshExecutionContext, host: HostIdentity, argv: List<String>) -> ArgvRun {
  match typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: fleet_locus_ssh_target(host: host), context: context, argv: argv) {
    TypedArgvExecRefused { reason: why } => ArgvLegDidNotRun { cause: why }
    TypedArgvExecConverged { result: r } => ArgvRan { exit_code: r.exit_code, stdout: r.stdout, stderr: r.stderr }
  }
}

// OBSERVE, THEN RELEASE. The observation is taken on the host after the effect's body returned,
// whatever it returned: a quiet host releases the claim; residue or an unread scan leaves the
// claim live and says so on the exit. Nothing here reads the exit as evidence.
type HostEffectSettlement
  = HostEffectSettled
  | HostEffectStillHeld { cause: String }

fn settle_host_effect_over(handle: HostEffectHandle, provenance: ReleaseProvenance, run: fn(List<String>) -> ArgvRun, store: FabricStorageBinding, executor: NonEmptyStr, at: EpochSecs) -> HostEffectSettlement {
  match observe_host_effect_quiescence(run: run, claim: handle.claim, host: handle.host, spec: handle.residue, observer: executor, at: at) {
    HostQuiescenceUnread { host: h, cause: c } => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: join([h as String, " could not be read for quiescence, so the host-effect claim ", handle.claim as String, " stays live: ", c], "") }
    HostResidue { host: h, detail: d } => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: join([h as String, " still carries the effect, so the host-effect claim ", handle.claim as String, " stays live: ", d], "") }
    HostQuiet { evidence: ev } =>
      match release_host_effect_over(handle: handle, evidence: ev, provenance: provenance, store: store, executor: executor, at: at) {
        Absent => HostEffectSettled
        Present { value: why } => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: why }
      }
  }
}

fn settle_host_effect_live(handle: HostEffectHandle) -> HostEffectSettlement {
  match observe_executor_reach() {
    ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: join(["the executor could not be identified for the release: ", c], "") }
    ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: executor, path: _ } =>
      match event_log_store_for_host(short_hostname: executor) {
        HostStoreRefused { detail: d } => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: d }
        HostStoreResolved { store: store, executor: _ } =>
          match now_epoch_seconds() {
            Absent => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: "the clock could not be read as epoch seconds for the release" }
            Present { value: at } =>
              match prepare_fleet_ssh_agent_context(attempt_raw: host_effect_release_attempt_raw) {
                FleetSshContextRefused { cause: c } => HostEffectStillHeld { cause: join(["the host could not be reached to observe quiescence, so the host-effect claim ", handle.claim as String, " stays live: ", c], "") }
                FleetSshContextReady { context: context, receipt: _ } =>
                  settle_host_effect_over(handle: handle, provenance: ClaimantTerminal { claim: handle.claim, claimant_executor: executor as NonEmptyStr }, run: fn(argv) { fleet_argv_run(context: context, host: handle.host, argv: argv) }, store: store, executor: executor as NonEmptyStr, at: at)
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn release_host_effect_live(handle: HostEffectHandle?, exit: ProcessExit) -> ProcessExit {
  match handle {
    Absent => exit
    Present { value: h } =>
      match settle_host_effect_live(handle: h) {
        HostEffectSettled => exit
        HostEffectStillHeld { cause: why } =>
          match exit {
            ExitSuccess => exit_failure(reason: why)
            ExitFailure { code: _, reason: r } => exit_failure(reason: join([r, "\n", why], ""))
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── RECOVERING A CRASHED LANE'S CLAIM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A lane that died between its claim and its release left the claim live, and nothing releases
// it on a clock. This entry is the authorized route, and it consumes TWO facts the normal
// terminal has by construction: the exact claim (never "whatever is live on the host"), and the
// claimant's standing -- terminated or abandoned -- as a typed operator disposition with the
// receipt that established it (a cancelled run's id, a dead process's last observation). A quiet
// host alone is not enough: a live lane between two legs is quiet too, and releasing it would
// admit another placement under it. The observation is then taken against THAT claim's residue,
// and the release lands only when the host is quiet.
//   gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/spark/host_commitment.dag
//     --function host_effect_recover_wet --arg host=<identity> --arg claim=<event id>
//     --arg claimant=terminated|abandoned --arg receipt=<how that was established>
fn host_effect_recover_wet(host: String, claim: String, claimant: String, receipt: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  if host == "" || claim == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "a host and the exact claim (event id) are required; this entry does not pick a claim by host")
  } else if receipt == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "a receipt establishing the claimant's standing is required (what showed the lane terminated or abandoned)")
  } else {
    match parse_claimant_standing(wire: claimant) {
      Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["`", claimant, "` is not a claimant standing; say terminated or abandoned"], ""))
      Present { value: standing } => {
        let h = host as HostIdentity
        let c = (claim as NonEmptyStr) as EventId
        match observe_executor_reach() {
          ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: cause } => exit_failure(reason: join(["the executor could not be identified: ", cause], ""))
          ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: executor, path: _ } =>
            match event_log_store_for_host(short_hostname: executor) {
              HostStoreRefused { detail: d } => exit_failure(reason: d)
              HostStoreResolved { store: store, executor: _ } =>
                match now_epoch_seconds() {
                  Absent => exit_failure(reason: "the clock could not be read as epoch seconds")
                  Present { value: at } => {
                    let provenance = RecoveryAuthorized { claim: c, claimant: standing, authorized_by: executor as NonEmptyStr, receipt: receipt as NonEmptyStr }
                    match live_claim_on(store: store, host: h, claim: c) {
                      LiveClaimUnread { step: st, reason: why } => exit_failure(reason: join(["the host-placement partition could not be read at ", st, ": ", why], ""))
                      LiveClaimAbsent => exit_failure(reason: join(["no live host-effect claim ", claim, " on ", host, "; nothing to recover"], ""))
                      LiveClaimFound { record: r } =>
                        match prepare_fleet_ssh_agent_context(attempt_raw: host_effect_release_attempt_raw) {
                          FleetSshContextRefused { cause: cause } => exit_failure(reason: cause)
                          FleetSshContextReady { context: context, receipt: _ } => {
                            let handle = HostEffectHandle { host: h, residue: r.residue, claim: r.id }
                            match settle_host_effect_over(handle: handle, provenance: provenance, run: fn(argv) { fleet_argv_run(context: context, host: h, argv: argv) }, store: store, executor: executor as NonEmptyStr, at: at) {
                              HostEffectSettled => ExitSuccess
                              HostEffectStillHeld { cause: why } => exit_failure(reason: why)
                            }
                          }
                        }
                    }
                  }
                }
            }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// The admission over the RESTING rows -- the pure projection, for consumers with no effect to
// admit. An effect asks admit_unplaced_host_live, which reads the current authority and claims.
fn admit_unplaced_host(raw: String, purpose: NonEmptyStr) -> SparkHostAdmission {
  admit_unplaced_host_among(
    raw: raw,
    purpose: purpose,
    unplaced: spark_unplaced_hosts,
    held_causes: h => spark_authority_held_causes_wire(host: h) as String,
  )
}
